.TH TRN 1 2000-1月 "open_bicor" "open_bicor"
.SH NAME
open_bicor \- re-open the persisted bicor correlation store that was written by `write_bicor`
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fIopen_bicor(\fBrepo\fR as string, 
\fBcache\fR as integer = 2048, 
[\fB<Environment>\fR]);\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
re-open the persisted bicor correlation store that was written by `write_bicor`
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fBrepo\fB \fR\- 
 the data repository directory that was passed to `write_bicor`
 (reads `{repo}/bicor.dat`, or `{repo}/pearson.dat` when the
 store was written with `corKind = "pearson"`, and
 `{repo}/index.dat`).
. 
.PP
.PP
\fBcache\fB \fR\- 
 the hot-row LRU cache capacity in rows. Each cached row of a N = 50,000
 matrix costs about 200 KB of memory. The default value 2048 means at most
 about 400 MB of the hot row cache.
. 
.PP
.PP
\fBenv\fB \fR\- the R# runtime environment object.. 
.PP
.SH VALUE
.PP
an opened [CorrelationMatrixStore](cref:T:Microsoft.VisualBasic.Math.Matrix.CorrelationMatrixStore) object. Pass it to
 `build_grn` via the `bicor` parameter to skip the expensive
 correlation matrix re-computation.
.PP
.SH DETAILS
.PP
the returned store supports fast point queries (gene1, gene2 -> correlation
 and p-value), neighborhood queries (gene + threshold -> all correlated genes)
 and full-library streaming edge filters, all without loading the matrix
 into memory.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
let bicor = open_bicor("Z:/hsa_mat");
.PP
